<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Prince Edward Island</h1><p class="st-meta">1769 CE – 1873 CE · capital Charlottetown</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Gulf of St Lawrence colony shaped by its absentee landlord system, and later the birthplace of talks that led to Canadian Confederation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Known to the French as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-edward-island">Île Saint-Jean</a>, the island passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> in 1763 and was attached to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>. In 1767 it was divided by lottery into 67 lots granted to absentee proprietors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and in 1769 it was made a separate colony with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-patterson">Walter Patterson</a> as governor. It was renamed Prince Edward Island in 1799.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony had an elected assembly from 1773 and responsible government from 1851. Its central problem was the land question: most farmers were tenants paying rent to proprietors overseas, and agitation by the Escheat movement and the Tenant League dominated politics. The economy rested on farming, fishing and shipbuilding, with many settlers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-highlands">Scottish Highlands</a>, Ireland and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/acadia">Acadia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlottetown-conference">Charlottetown Conference</a> of 1864 launched Confederation, but the island at first stayed out of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-canada">Canada</a>. Debt from a costly railway, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa's</a> promise to buy out the absentee landlords, brought it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> as a province in 1873.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colony-of-prince-edward-island.jpg" alt="Colony of Prince Edward Island" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Image: E Pluribus Anthony · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Prince_Edward_Island.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1769 CE – 1873 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Charlottetown</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Walter Patterson</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism, Presbyterianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Scottish Gaelic, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>St. John's Island, Prince Edward Island</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1821&amp;focus=colony-of-prince-edward-island" aria-label="Colony of Prince Edward Island on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3191 551 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of Prince Edward Island lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3511 807L3483 855M3511 807L4997 651M3511 807L3203 831"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3483 855h0M4997 651h0M3203 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3511 807h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3511 807h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Charlottetown. <a href="/atlas?year=1821&amp;focus=colony-of-prince-edward-island">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
